@* Settings — the home the app's two controls never had.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Language and theme used to live in the tail of the navigation list, below
   the destinations. That was a housing decision made because the list is the
   same element at both breakpoints and a row added to it collapses on a phone
   for free — not a claim that a control belongs among sections. This screen is
   the home they were missing, so the nav becomes destinations-only and the
   exception in its comment closes.

   PUBLIC, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Appearance and language are choices about how
   the app is read, not about what it will show you — a signed-out visitor has
   exactly as much reason to change them as a signed-in one, and gating them
   would gate the app's own legibility behind a simulated session.

   Signed in, two more fields appear. There is no "sign in to see more" prompt
   in their place: the two visible fields are complete and useful on their own,
   and announcing the absence of the other two would manufacture a gap that does
   not need calling out.

   NO ROLE FIELD, DELIBERATELY. A holder's track is a derived precondition, not
   a preference — Track.cs documents that there is no way to assign one and that
   this is intentional. A Settings row that let a person pick their own track
   would invent an entitlement system this app does not have and must not
   imply. *@

@implements IDisposable
@page "/settings"
@inject SimulatedSessionProvider Session
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject LocaleService L

<PageTitle>@L[Locale, "settings.title"]</PageTitle>

<h1>@L[Locale, "settings.title"]</h1>

<div class="settings">
    @* Four fields do not need internal grouping, so there are no section
       headings under the h1 — inventing structure the content does not ask for
       is how a settings screen becomes a settings menu. *@
    <AppearanceControl />

    <LanguageSelect />

    @if (Session.IsSignedIn)
    {
        @* Read-only, and not an oversight. There is no account system behind
           this session to write a new name to, so an editable field would be
           the interface lying about what it is.

           GENERIC RATHER THAN THE ACCOUNT'S OWN NAME. Both demo accounts carry
           a persona internally, but nothing on screen names either one — a
           reader signed in to either sees the same label here, which is
           correct: this is a generic test account, not somebody's identity. *@
        <div class="settings-field">
            <span class="settings-field__label">@L[Locale, "settings.name"]</span>
            <p class="settings-field__value">@L[Locale, "record.genericHolderName"]</p>
        </div>

        <div class="settings-field">
            @* The second of the two sign-out locations, the first being the
               session row above the content column. A duplicated status
               indicator can disagree with itself; a duplicated action cannot.
               Both call the same method and land in the same place. *@
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary settings__signout" @onclick="SignOut">
                @L[Locale, "settings.signOut"]
            </button>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public AppLocale Locale { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Session.OnChanged += Refresh;

    public void Dispose() => Session.OnChanged -= Refresh;

    /// <summary>
    /// Sign-out can also happen from the session row above the content column,
    /// which is not this component's parent and cannot tell it directly.
    /// </summary>
    private void Refresh() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void SignOut()
    {
        Session.SignOut();

        // Home, which announces that the sign-out happened rather than leaving
        // a person to infer it from a screen that quietly lost two fields.
        Navigation.NavigateTo("");
    }
}
